STUDY#S#SWELL
I#R#ERASE#G#E
LUNGS#R#NIGHT
EN#A#ACE#M#CT
NOOSE#A#APPLE
C#D#LASTS#A#R
ENDED#M#HOLES


A1. "We'll flip a coin," said the college boys, "To see what we'll do to-night, Heads: It's movies; tails: It's cards And this if it stands upright." ~ STUDY
A5. He's got a ritzy face to-day; His mug is full of bumps; For all jaws do this when They catch a few stray mumps. ~ SWELL
A8. She "puzzled" away but never got on. Oh! She was most perplexed! As soon as she would write a word Why here's what she'd do next! ~ ERASE
A9. Frank and Mitch, they had it out, They could not argue longer. And Mitch, he won the verbal bout. See! Mitchell's were the stronger. ~ LUNGS
A12. Take out your watch! What time's it when A Scotsman, dressed in tweed, Runs panting, followed in the dark By a burly, blond-haired Swede? ~ NIGHT
A15. Both a suffix and a prefix; Of the alphabet, a letter; Besides; a printer's measure. Hints? There are no better. ~ EN
A16. Majesty in England still holds sway, They think it's quite the thing. King George ranks high. For Englishmen What's higher than a king? ~ ACE
A17. Abbreviate some words and Their value is not spent. Here's a word, shortened, which, Is worth just 'bout a cent. ~ CT
A18. "I'll be getting," said the thief, As he had his head in it, "The real hang of the thing In just about a minute." ~ NOOSE
A21. Eve ate of it, and gave Adam, He was easy to convince. He took a bite and choked outright, It's stuck in his throat since. ~ APPLE
A23. My old black shoes came off of them! (You may think this very dumb!) If so try this—the word applies To the flavor of Wrigley's gum. ~ LASTS
A24. A girl's happiness is —, When Miss she changes to Mrs. You'll be real happy though When this is. ~ ENDED
A25. If you bought some fancy hosiery, With nicely woven clocks, You'd hate like everything to find These things in your socks. ~ HOLES

D1. Some things are very hard to smashr. A diamond; so I've heard. But here's a thing that you will break If you just say one word. ~ SILENCE
D2. They baked him to a cinder, Inside an iron closet. And all of his two hundred pounds, In this, they did deposit. ~ URN
D3. Here's a bughouse fable; "Applesauce," you'll say. He asked her just once for a kiss, Here's what she said straightway. ~ YES
D4. For a word harshly spoken, 'Tis hard to make amends! Verily here's the chasm which Oftimes lies 'twixt friends! ~ SARCASM
D5. A large coin made of copper, And worth a cent, perhaps. The only things worth while in life, According to the Japs. ~ SEN
D6. He wants to act no longer now, The stage he doesn't miss! He thought he'd gather lots of dough; He collected only this. ~ EGG
D7. Izzy de Bear was a millionaire, A man of wealth and fame! While Jonathan Fall was nothing at all. Ho! Ho! What's in a name? ~ LETTERS
D10. From many other languages Our U. S. speech has sprung. We've garnered many foreign words; Here's one in Italy's tongue. ~ UNO
D11. "A pipe," he asked with manner cool, She smiled and gave assent; She thought him a conceited fool; This is the kind she sent. ~ GAS
D13. A supernatural being; A very mischievous lad; There's always a few dancing Along a Wrigley's ad. ~ IMP
D14. Volumes of troubles I express As these letters three, I sigh; I can't afford to live, and still It costs too much to die! ~ HCL
D19. Like the number of its letters So is this easy word. It would be this if over this Your brains should become stirred. ~ ODD
D20. An old poetical version of A word that's really old, With such hints you can't but guess— And that's all you'll be told. ~ ELD
D21. Incombustible, non-volatile residue Of an organic substance burnt. Might sound high-hat, but in college That is what I learnt. ~ ASH
D22. I thought she'd be, but this to me, Our friendship was Platonic. Philosophic kisses, turned her to Mrs., Our friendship now is chronic. ~ PAL


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
